#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166462 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca890a8e-9ddd-51f7-9784-c9c1f020adef" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Laplace Transform to a System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "008364d3-24bd-58e2-a8ab-f0d4d881e918" :question/math-academy-id "e-21575" :question/problem "${x_{1}^{′}(t) = 2x_{1}(t) + 5\\cos (2t) - 3; x_{2}^{′}(t) =-3x_{1}(t) + 4x_{2}(t) + 2\\sin (2t)$,\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, find the system of equations." :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n{L{x_{1}^{′}(t)} = L{2x_{1}(t) + 5\\cos (2t) - 3}; L{x_{2}^{′}(t)} = L{-3x_{1}(t) + 4x_{2}(t) + 2\\sin (2t)}\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{sL{x_{1}(t)} - x_{1}(0) = 2L{x_{1}(t)} + 5L{\\cos (2t)} - 3L{1}; sL{x_{2}(t)} - x_{2}(0) =-3L{x_{1}(t)} + 4L{x_{2}(t)} + 2L{\\sin (2t)}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the results\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{1\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s}, \\quad \\mathcal L\\{\\cos(2t)\\} = \\dfrac{s}{s^2+4}, \\quad \\mathcal L\\{\\sin(2t)\\} = \\dfrac{2}{s^2+4}\n$$\n\nwe can write our system as follows:\n\n$$\n{sX_{1}(s) - 1 = 2X_{1}(s) + \\frac{5s}{s^{2} + 4} - \\frac{3}{s}; sX_{2}(s) + 2 =-3X_{1}(s) + 4X_{2}(s) + \\frac{4}{s^{2} + 4}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nX_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_1(t) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nX_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_2(t) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nare the Laplace transforms of the functions $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$, respectively."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166463 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b22e449-2966-59cf-b012-3f5721b4bc98" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution of a Laplace Transformed Initial Value Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "230e2550-b584-53fd-9da4-4128584124dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-21576" :question/problem "${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-x_{1}(t) + 4; x_{2}^{′}(t) = 2x_{1}(t) - 3x_{2}(t)$,\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $\\mathcal{L}$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables $X_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_1(t) \\right\\}$ and $X_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_2(t) \\right\\}$. In terms of the parameter $s$, find the solution of the new system." :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n{L{x_{1}^{′}(t)} = L{-x_{1}(t) + 4}; L{x_{2}^{′}(t)} = L{2x_{1}(t) - 3x_{2}(t)}\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{sL{x_{1}(t)} - x_{1}(0) =-L{x_{1}(t)} + 4L{1}; sL{x_{2}(t)} - x_{2}(0) = 2L{x_{1}(t)} - 3L{x_{2}(t)}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n{sX_{1}(s) - 1 =-X_{1}(s) + \\frac{4}{s}; sX_{2}(s) - 0 = 2X_{1}(s) - 3X_{2}(s)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nX_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_1(t) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nX_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_2(t) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nand we have used the fact that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{1\\} = \\dfrac1s\n$$\n\nNow, solving the first equation for $X_1(s)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nsX_{1}(s) - 1 &= -X_{1}(s) + \\frac{4}{s} \\\\\n(s + 1)X_{1}(s) &= 1 + \\frac{4}{s} \\\\\n(s + 1)X_{1}(s) &= \\frac{s + 4}{s} \\\\\nX_{1}(s) &= (s + 4)/(s(s + 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the second equation and solving for $X_2(s)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nsX_{2}(s) &= 2X_{1}(s) - 3X_{2}(s) \\\\\n(s + 3)X_{2}(s) &= 2X_{1}(s) \\\\\n(s + 3)X_{2}(s) &= 2((s + 4)/(s(s + 1))) \\\\\nX_{2}(s) &= (2(s + 4))/(s(s + 1)(s + 3))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the new system in terms of parameter $s$ is\n\n$$\nX_1(s) = \\dfrac{s+4}{s(s+1)},\\qquad X_2(s) = \\dfrac{2(s+4)}{s(s+1)(s+3)}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166464 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff314a2-9059-5a37-8c2c-2b7a2fe6dcd9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the Laplace Transformed Solution"}] :question/id #uuid "748ef205-542b-5763-9cdd-00dc9ff1d181" :question/math-academy-id "e-21577" :question/problem "${x_{1}^{′}(t) = x_{1}(t) + 3x_{2}(t) - 7; x_{2}^{′}(t) =-2x_{2}(t) + 6$,\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution\n$X_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_1(t) \\right\\} = -\\dfrac{1}{s+2} + \\dfrac{2}{s-1} - \\dfrac{2}{s}, \\qquad X_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_2(t) \\right\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s+2} + \\dfrac{3}{s}$.\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem." :question/worked-solution "To find $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to $X_1(s)$ and $X_2(s){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}(t) &= L^{-1}{X_{1}(s)} \\\\\n&= L^{-1}{-\\frac{1}{s + 2} + \\frac{2}{s - 1} - \\frac{2}{s}} \\\\\n&=-L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s + 2}} + 2L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s - 1}} - 2L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}} \\\\\n&=-e^{-2 \\cdot t} + 2e^{1 \\cdot t} - 2 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 2e^{t} - e^{-2t} - 2 \\\\\nx_{2}(t) &= L^{-1}{X_{2}(s)} \\\\\n&= L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s + 2} + \\frac{3}{s}} \\\\\n&= L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s + 2}} + 3L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}} \\\\\n&= e^{-2 \\cdot t} + 3 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= e^{-2t} + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}(t) \\\\ x_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2e^{t} - e^{-2t} - 2 \\\\ e^{-2t} + 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167127 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff314a2-9059-5a37-8c2c-2b7a2fe6dcd9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the Laplace Transformed Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "a493890a-54c1-5f74-8e10-1840b039d796" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164123} {:answer-field/id #uuid "c0a025f7-5895-5e9d-8fec-3c91e3365f8d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164124}] :question/id #uuid "05245fe3-011d-5dbc-b60f-1afb740f2732" :question/math-academy-id "q-330958" :question/problem "${x_{1}^{′}(t) = x_{1}(t) + 3; x_{2}^{′}(t) =-x_{1}(t) + 3x_{2}(t),x(0) = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution\n$X_{1}(s) = L{x_{1}(t)} = \\frac{1}{s - 1} - \\frac{3}{s}, X_{2}(s) = L{x_{2}(t)} = \\frac{1/2}{s - 1} - \\frac{1}{s} + \\frac{3/2}{s - 3}$.\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n| $x(t) =$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |  |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167170 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b22e449-2966-59cf-b012-3f5721b4bc98" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution of a Laplace Transformed Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "d908a6ad-bb07-5b6f-b150-d8b540c4eaef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164431}] :question/id #uuid "968c34ea-6536-56be-a722-82e75e44e26e" :question/math-academy-id "q-333064" :question/problem "${x_{1}^{′}(t) = x_{1}(t) + 2x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = 3x_{2}(t) + 4,x(0) = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables $X_{1}(s) = L{x_{1}(t)}$ and $X_{2}(s) = L{x_{2}(t)}$. In terms of the parameter $s$, the solution of the new system is$X_{1}(s) =$ {{field-1}} $$,\n$X_{2}(s) = (2s + 4)/(s(s - 3))$.\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"solving-an-initial-value-problem-given-the-laplace-transformed-solution\"></a>"}]}
